While I go for the idea of 'controlling' polypiling in some way, I
would hate to see it eliminated.  In some games, it is the only way to
get absolutely essential items.  If it were to be eliminated, then
there should be some other mechanism added to the game to ensure that
required items would be generated somewhere.

Which leads to a more general gripe of mine, which is that there should
be some form of 'safety latch' on randomness, to ensure that things
which are necessary will occur/appear in sensible time.  (I.e., the
game should always be possible to win, IF played correctly.  My
propensity for getting myself killed is sufficiently great that I don't
really need help in the form of impossible games.)

This is floating around in my mind right now because of a game I
'played' recently, where I died on the first level.  I started in a
room with no doors, 1 pet, one monster.  The monster instantly attacked
and killed my pet (leaving no corpse).  I killed the monster (leaving a
corpse, which I ate) and began searching for a door.  I searched the
room randomly, and then systematically, until I starved to death,
without ever finding a door, a useful trap (teleport or trapdoor), or
having a generated monster appear with any helpful artifacts.  Praying
got me an 'I'm pissed off with you' type message.  Annoyed the hell out
of me.

Such extreme instances are rare (in my experience, at least) but lesser
forms of the problem still crop up too often (IMHO).  If something is
necessary to complete the game (in this case, a door) then the game would
play better if there were some guarantee that you would be able to find
it, if you are careful and thorough.

